  <p>
    You have decided to set up a YumePad for your elderly parent. The conversation is done, the questions have been answered — or at least the most important ones — and the box has arrived. Now comes the part that no product manual quite captures: the practical reality of introducing a new piece of technology into an elderly person's home, on their terms, in a way that leaves them feeling more secure rather than surveilled, more loved rather than managed.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide covers the complete first-night setup process: the physical installation, the Bluetooth pairing, the family invite, the baseline learning period, and — perhaps most importantly — the conversation with your parent about what the device actually does and does not do. It also covers what to expect in the first 24 hours versus the first week, and how to interpret what you see in the app before the system has built a reliable personal baseline.
  </p>

  <h2>Before You Open the Box: Have the Conversation First</h2>
  <p>
    The most common mistake families make when setting up the YumePad for an elderly parent is doing it backwards: installing the hardware first, then explaining what it does. This sequence, however well-intentioned, communicates something unintended — that the child's agenda takes precedence over the parent's understanding. The conversation should come first, even if the conversation happened weeks ago when you first proposed the idea. Before the box opens, revisit the key points, briefly and warmly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The conversation has three parts. First: what the YumePad does. It is a thin sensor that goes under the mattress and measures body signals — heart rate, breathing, movement — while your parent sleeps. It does not have a camera. It does not record sound. It cannot "watch" anyone. Second: what the data is used for. The app gives your parent a sleep quality report each morning. If your parent chooses to share access with you, you will see a summary of how they slept — not live footage, not a constant feed, just a nightly summary. Third: what it does not do. It is not a replacement for phone calls, visits, or medical care. It is one additional piece of information in an ongoing relationship, not a substitute for that relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many elderly people, when given this explanation, express a concern that sounds like resistance but is actually something else: "I don't want to be a burden." They worry that their sleep data will cause their children to worry more, not less. The reassuring answer — and the honest one — is that the opposite tends to be true. Knowing that a parent is sleeping normally gives adult children the information they need to sleep normally themselves. The absence of alarming information is itself reassuring, night after night.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>"I was worried it would make my son hover more. It turned out to make him hover less. He could see I was fine, so he stopped imagining I wasn't."</p>
    <footer>— YumePad user, 78, Tokyo</footer>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Physical Installation: Under the Mattress, Not the Pad</h2>
  <p>
    The YumePad installs in under five minutes. The sensor pad — approximately 60 cm × 40 cm, roughly the size of a small pillow — slides under the mattress, centered beneath the sleeping area of your parent's torso. The pad should sit between the mattress and the bed base (or box spring, slats, or platform frame), not between the mattress and the fitted sheet. The sensor needs the mattress's weight above it to detect the micro-vibrations of heartbeat and breathing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Positioning matters. The sensor zone of the YumePad is located in the center-upper third of the pad; this should be positioned beneath where your parent's chest and upper abdomen will rest when sleeping in their normal position. If your parent tends to sleep in the center of the bed, center the pad accordingly. If they habitually sleep closer to one side, offset the pad to keep the sensor zone under their sleeping position.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pad connects to a small hub unit — roughly the size of a large matchbox — via a thin cable that runs along the side of the bed. The hub plugs into a standard power outlet and connects via Bluetooth to the app on your parent's smartphone (or your smartphone, if your parent does not use one). The hub does not need to be hidden, but most families find that tucking it behind or beside a bedside table keeps the setup tidy. The cable is low-profile and can be secured with the included adhesive clips if needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once the pad is in position and the hub is powered, a single green LED on the hub confirms the sensor is receiving signal. If the LED blinks amber, the pad is not detecting a sufficient signal — usually because it needs to be repositioned slightly closer to the center of the sleeping zone, or because the mattress is unusually thick or dense. The troubleshooting guide in the app walks through the three most common positioning issues in under two minutes.
  </p>

