  <h2>Bluetooth Pairing via the App</h2>
  <p>
    Download the SleepTech app on the device that will serve as the primary paired device — typically your parent's smartphone if they have one, or yours if the setup will be managed primarily by the family. Open the app and tap "Add New Device." The app will guide you through Bluetooth pairing with the hub; the process takes approximately 90 seconds and requires no technical knowledge. The hub will be named automatically using the last four characters of its serial number; you can rename it in the app settings to something like "Mum's Room" for clarity.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your parent uses an Android device running Android 10 or older, ensure that Location permission is granted to the app — this is a system-level requirement for Bluetooth device discovery on older Android versions and does not mean the app uses your location for any other purpose. iOS devices from iPhone 7 onward and Android 11 and later do not require this permission.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once paired, the app will ask you to confirm the sleeping side — whether your parent sleeps in a shared bed (in which case, indicate which side of the bed they occupy) or alone. For shared-bed configurations, a second YumePad can be added for the partner; the app handles both sensor streams simultaneously and can generate separate reports for each sleeper while also providing a combined view for the bedroom.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting Up the Family Invite</h2>
  <p>
    The family invite is the feature that transforms the YumePad from a personal sleep tracker into a caregiving tool. From the app's main menu, your parent navigates to Settings → Family Sharing → Invite a Family Member. They enter your email address (or phone number, if you prefer to receive notifications via SMS). You receive an invitation link, accept it, and from that point, you will receive the nightly summary that your parent has chosen to share.
  </p>
  <p>
    The invitation flow is deliberately designed to be initiated by the elderly person, not by the family member. This is not a technical constraint — it would be easy to design it the other way — but a deliberate choice that reflects SleepTech's data governance principles. The elderly person is the data owner. They invite you in. They can revoke access at any time from the same Settings menu, without requiring any action on your part.
  </p>
  <p>
    When setting up the shared view, your parent can also configure which alert types are sent to you. The options include: nightly summary (always available), sleep quality anomaly alerts (when a night significantly deviates from baseline), bed-exit duration alerts (for fall prevention — see separate article), and heart rate anomaly alerts (when resting heart rate during sleep significantly deviates from baseline). Most families begin with nightly summary plus sleep quality anomaly alerts, adding bed-exit alerts if fall risk is a specific concern.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will not receive real-time access to the raw sensor stream. What you receive is a human-readable summary — "Your parent slept 6h 42m last night, with good deep sleep in the first half of the night, and woke briefly twice around 3 AM. No anomalies detected." — plus any alert notifications that were triggered. This is intentional: a live data feed creates anxiety rather than reducing it, and the summary format is designed to answer the question you actually want answered, not every question the data could theoretically support.
  </p>

  <h2>The Baseline Learning Period: 3 to 7 Nights</h2>
  <p>
    The most important thing to understand about the first week with YumePad is that the system is learning. Every individual has a unique sleep physiology — characteristic heart rate patterns during different sleep stages, typical respiratory rate variability, habitual sleep and wake times, normal movement patterns during the night. The YumePad builds a personal baseline model from the first seven nights of data, and this baseline is what makes subsequent anomaly detection meaningful.
  </p>
  <p>
    During this learning period, the data the app shows you is accurate — the sleep staging, heart rate, and breathing rate figures reflect what the sensor is actually measuring — but the anomaly detection and trend analysis features are disabled or clearly marked as "insufficient baseline." You may see the sleep quality score fluctuate more than you would expect from week two onward. This is normal: the system is building the model, and early-night data has higher uncertainty than data accumulated over a week or more.
  </p>
  <p>
    The baseline period is also when the system learns the specific characteristics of the sleeping environment: the noise floor of the sensor in that particular bed frame, the vibration signature of any HVAC or fan systems in the room, the movement patterns of a partner if the bed is shared. These environmental calibration parameters persist in the hub's local memory and are updated gradually over time, meaning the system continues to improve its signal-to-noise discrimination throughout the device's lifetime.
  </p>
  <p>
    Families sometimes ask whether the baseline period can be shortened. The answer is: the minimum reliable baseline is three nights for the basic sleep staging model, and seven nights for the full anomaly detection model. If the first three nights show consistent signal quality, anomaly detection for very significant deviations (large heart rate changes, very long bed-exit events) will be available from night four. The full sensitivity of the anomaly detection system requires the seven-night baseline. Rushing this period by reducing the detection thresholds increases false-positive alert rates, which is counterproductive.
  </p>

